Edit 3: the new warning fn. Place it right after `malformed_hook_key_warning`.

```rust
/// Pure: warn when a `hooks.<phase>.<tool>` key names a phase that is not in
/// [`HookPhase::ALL`].
///
/// This is the difference between a typo a user can fix and a hook that
/// silently never fires: `hooks.postfailure.bash = "..."` used to parse to
/// nothing at all, with no message anywhere. Scope matters and is deliberate —
/// the warning fires **only** for a key that has both a phase segment and a
/// tool segment, so a bare `hooks.<name>` key (which may be a config key of
/// some future non-hook kind) keeps `malformed_hook_key_warning`'s silence.
/// The accepted set is read from [`HookPhase::ALL`], never re-listed here.
fn unknown_hook_phase_warning(rest: &str, plain: bool) -> Option<String> {
    let (segment, _tool) = rest.split_once('.')?;
    if HookPhase::parse(segment).is_some() {
        return None;
    }

    let accepted = HookPhase::ALL
        .iter()
        .map(|p| format!("`hooks.{}.<tool>`", p.as_str()))
        .collect::<Vec<_>>()
        .join(", ");
    let marker = if plain { "warning:" } else { "⚠" };
    let joiner = if plain { ";" } else { " —" };
    Some(format!(
        "{marker} hook key `hooks.{rest}` names no known phase, so it can never \
         fire{joiner} accepted phases are {accepted}."
    ))
}
```

Note the `?` on split_once: returns None when no dot → silent. 

